AT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues.\n\n* WHERE...West Central Montezuma County in southwestern Colorado...\nSoutheastern San Juan County in southeastern Utah along the San\nJuan River from the Four Corners to Lake Powell...\n\n* WHEN...Until 345 PM MDT.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is occurring. The San Juan River will\ncontinue to rise this afternoon through Bluff and Mexican Hat.\nProjected peak will occur around midnight tonight.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 143 PM MDT, River forecasts estimate a peak on the San Juan\nRiver near Bluff to exceed 25 feet tonight, with a secondary peak\nexpected overnight Saturday into Sunday. Gauge reports indicated\nhigh accumulations of rainfall since Thursday. Flooding is\nongoing in the warned area.\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nTohache Wash, San Juan River, Toh Dahstini Wash and Cowboy Wash.\nFlooding impacts will continue, but no additional rainfall is\nexpected.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nMexican Hat, Tselakai Dezza, Bluff, Halchita, Montezuma Creek and\nAneth.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T15:45:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Montezuma, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9fc5ea75f369e91ca0294c0cdcbc1231828d2450.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9fc5ea75f369e91ca0294c0cdcbc1231828d2450.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 23 at 11:59AM MDT until October 25 at 12:00PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and Utah,\nincluding the following areas, Arches/Grand Flat,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through Sunday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recent burn scars and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Flooding is imminent or ongoing with additional rounds of showers\nand thunderstorms expected through Saturday afternoon. Rivers and\ncreeks may still experience high waters even after rainfall has\nended.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-25T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; Arches/Grand Flat; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a28b19253bb7550e7a42b701dae47ec9af4c8ee0.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a28b19253bb7550e7a42b701dae47ec9af4c8ee0.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T20:04:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 232004 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 204 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Flooding is ongoing across portions of southeast Utah and southwest Colorado, and is expected to continue through Saturday. - Many rivers will experience their crest Friday or Saturday. - Isolated to scattered showers and storms are expected Friday and Sat","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T06:36:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED N